---
title: "stdlib.crypto Tutorial"
author: wesleygitari2-cloud
date: 2026-11-23
abstract: Complete tutorial for the LATERALUS stdlib.crypto module. Covers hash functions (SHA-256/512, MD5, BLAKE2b), HMAC sign/verify, hex and Base64 codecs, and PBKDF2 password hashing — with pinned test vectors verified against the Python backend.

# stdlib.crypto Tutorial

The `crypto` module (`lateralus_lang/crypto_engine.py`, imported as `import crypto;`)
provides cryptographic primitives: one-way hash functions, HMAC message
authentication, hex/Base64 encoding, secure random generation, and
password hashing.

This tutorial covers the string/bool/dict-returning API — every snippet
below was executed with `python -m lateralus_lang run` on current main.

## Prerequisites

- LATERALUS with the Python backend (`PYTHONPATH=. python -m lateralus_lang run file.ltl`)
- No third-party packages needed for the functions covered here

## Quick Start

```lateralus
import crypto;

fn main() -> int {
    // SHA-256 of "abc" — FIPS 180-4 known answer
    println(crypto.sha256("abc"));
    // ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad

    // Base64 encode/decode round-trip
    println(crypto.to_base64("hi"));        // aGk=
    println(crypto.from_base64("aGk="));    // hi
    return 0;
}
```

---

## Hash Functions

All hashes return lowercase hex strings.

| Function | Output | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `crypto.sha256(data)` | 64 hex chars | FIPS 180-4 |
| `crypto.sha512(data)` | 128 hex chars | FIPS 180-4 |
| `crypto.md5(data)` | 32 hex chars | RFC 1321 (not collision-resistant — checksums only) |
| `crypto.blake2b(data, digest_size)` | hex, `digest_size` bytes | BLAKE2b, tunable output |
| `crypto.hash_data(data, algorithm)` | hex | dispatch by name: `"sha256"`, `"sha512"`, `"blake2b"`, `"md5"` |

Pinned vectors (verified):

```lateralus
crypto.sha256("abc")
// ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad

crypto.md5("abc")
// 900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72

crypto.sha512("abc")
// ddaf35a193617abacc417349ae20413112e6fa4e89a97ea20a9eeee64b55d39a2192992a274fc1a836ba3c23a3feebbd454d4423643ce80e2a9ac94fa54ca49f

crypto.blake2b("abc", 16)
// cf4ab791c62b8d2b2109c90275287816   (16-byte digest → 32 hex chars)

crypto.hash_data("abc", "md5")
// 900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72
```

---

## HMAC — keyed message authentication

`hmac_sign` returns a hex signature; `hmac_verify` does a constant-time
comparison and returns `bool`.

```lateralus
let sig = crypto.hmac_sign("key", "msg", "sha256");
// 2d93cbc1be167bcb1637a4a23cbff01a7878f0c50ee833954ea5221bb1b8c628

crypto.hmac_verify("key", "msg", sig, "sha256");   // true
crypto.hmac_verify("key", "msg", "00", "sha256");   // false
```

The `algorithm` parameter accepts any `hashlib` name (`"sha256"`,
`"sha512"`, `"sha1"`, ...). Default is `"sha256"`.

---

## Encoding helpers

```lateralus
crypto.to_hex("hi")            // "6869"
crypto.from_hex("6869")        // "hi"
crypto.to_base64("hi")         // "aGk="
crypto.from_base64("aGk=")     // "hi"

// Round-trip
crypto.from_base64(crypto.to_base64("round-trip me"))
// "round-trip me"
```

`to_base64`/`from_base64` handle UTF-8 strings; binary-safe variants take
`bytes` (see source for `xor_encrypt`, `random_bytes`, `lbe_*`).

---

## Password hashing (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256)

Never store raw passwords. `hash_password` returns a dict with
`hash`, `salt`, `iterations`, `algorithm`; `verify_password` checks a
candidate against it.

```lateralus
let stored = crypto.hash_password("hunter2");
// {
//   "hash":       "<64 hex>",
//   "salt":       "<64 hex>",
//   "iterations": 600000,
//   "algorithm":  "pbkdf2_sha256"
// }

crypto.verify_password("hunter2", stored);   // true
crypto.verify_password("wrong",   stored);   // false
```

Default work factor is 600,000 PBKDF2 iterations. Each call uses a fresh
random salt, so two `hash_password("x")` calls never produce the same dict.

---

## What is NOT covered here

- `random_token`, `random_bytes`, `random_urlsafe` — return `bytes`;
  printing them in the Python backend shows a Python `b'...'` repr.
  Use them for key material, not display.
- `aes_encrypt`/`aes_decrypt` — require the optional `cryptography`
  package (`_check_cryptography()` raises if absent).
- `xor_encrypt`, `lbe_encode`/`lbe_decode` — `bytes` in, `bytes` out;
  same repr caveat as above.
- `checksum_file`/`verify_file` — convenience wrappers over
  `hash_data` for file paths.

## Runnable example + pinned vectors

`examples/crypto_tutorial.ltl` exercises every function above with
known-answer checks (FIPS/NIST vectors) and prints `PASS`/`FAIL` per
check. Run it:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=. python -m lateralus_lang run examples/crypto_tutorial.ltl
# Expected tail: ALL CHECKS PASSED
```

Verified on main @ 2026-11-23: 16/16 checks pass.
